ToasterCG for AROS  1.0 RC7 (release candidate 7)
=================================================

The Video Toaster CG, NewTek's character generator, running natively on
AROS: the 4.1 interface as it was, drawn clean at any screen size, with
the books, fonts, brushes and ARexx scripts of the Video Toaster 4.

Nothing of the Video Toaster is in this archive. The CG draws its panel
from the CG program of your own Video Toaster and takes its fonts,
brushes, books and scripts from there.


What you need
-------------
- AROS x86_64 (or i386: use the i386 archive).
- reqtools.library and freetype2.library (both come with AROS).
- A Video Toaster 4.x installation (not part of this archive), assigned
  as Toaster: - its drawer as on the Video Toaster 4.x discs:
    Programs/CG                      the CG program (Toaster CG 4.1): the
                                     panel's gadgets and colours are read
                                     from it
    Programs/System_Support/Fonts/CGInterface/14.4C
                                     the panel's font (also looked for as
                                     Fonts/CGInterface/14.4C)
    Programs/CG_Support/cg-config    the macro keys, paths and devices
    ARexx/CG                         the CG's ARexx scripts
    PSFonts, Brushes, CG (Books, Keys, Scrolls, Crawls, Frames,
    TextFiles), Framestore           what books are made of: they name
                                     their fonts and brushes as
                                     Toaster:PSFonts/..., Toaster:Brushes/...
  The files are read, never changed. The CG was made with, and tested
  against, the CG of the Video Toaster 4.2 (Flyer 4.2 CD).
- For the ARexx scripts and macro keys (Alt-F1..F10): an ARexx interpreter
  that provides the REXX port, such as Regina with its RexxMast. AROS
  itself ships RX but no interpreter. cgrx (below) needs none.
  Regina as AROS builds it (contrib/regina) has two faults these scripts
  meet: every call to a function host added with addlib, as the CG's
  scripts all do, stops with "Assertion (FALSE) failed in amifuncs.c"
  (a "break;" is missing after the RRT_HOST case), and REMLIB is not a
  built-in function, so a script's REMLIB('CG_AREXX') ends it with
  Error 94. With both fixed the Toaster's scripts run (SaveState.Rexx
  and StartUp.rexx tested).


Installing
----------
Unpack the archive anywhere, with any LhA or with the lha of AROS's
contrib (Extras/Misc/aminet/C), for example into Work:

    CD Work:
    lha x ToasterCG-1.0RC7-x86_64.lha

Then point Toaster: at the Video Toaster's drawer, for example in
S:User-Startup:

    Assign Toaster: Work:Toaster

The first start draws the panel: the CG reads the gadget pictures and
the font from the Toaster's CG program and redraws them as outlines, so
they stay sharp at any size. That takes some seconds (9 on x86_64, 14 on
i386 in the emulator this was tested in; a small window shows how far it
is) and is done once: the result is kept as CG.art beside the
CG, or as T:ToasterCG.art when its drawer cannot be written to (then it
is drawn again after the next restart of the system). Later starts read
that file and need the Toaster's CG program no more; if the program or
the font is replaced by another, the panel is drawn again.

Without the CG program and its font (and without a CG.art) the CG says
which files it looked for and does not start. Without PSFonts it starts,
says so, and text has no fonts until one is loaded.


Running
-------
Double-click the CG icon, or from a shell:

    CG [BOOK] [TOASTER dir] [SCALE percent] [CONFIG file]

    BOOK     a book to open
    TOASTER  the Video Toaster's drawer, where Toaster: in books points
             (default Toaster:)
    SCALE    the window against the Toaster's 752x480 frame: 100 or more,
             in steps of 25; by default the largest that fits the screen
             (425 on a 3840x2160 screen)
    CONFIG   the cg-config file (default: cg-config beside the CG if
             there is one, else the Toaster's Programs/CG_Support/cg-config)

From Workbench the same are the icon's tool types (TOASTER=, SCALE=,
CONFIG=); shift-click a book's icon with the CG's to open it. The
window's size gadget resizes it in the same 25 percent steps.

The Toaster's cg-config names its macros as REXX:CG/..., where the
Toaster's installer copies the scripts. Where REXX:CG does not hold a
macro, the CG runs it from the Toaster's ARexx/CG instead. Three of the
scripts (RexxLauncher, RmWord, SpellCheck) look for other scripts and
their own files in REXX:CG themselves; for those, copy ARexx/CG there:

    Copy Toaster:ARexx/CG REXX:CG ALL

To change the macro keys, copy the Toaster's cg-config beside the CG
and edit the copy. A macro named PROGDIR:... in it is one in the CG's
drawer: while it runs the CG assigns ToasterCG: to its drawer (unless
that name is taken) and gives the interpreter such a macro as
ToasterCG:..., since it takes the first word of a macro's name as the
macro and a path such as "RAM Disk:..." has a space. For the same
reason the Toaster's drawer should be reached by a name without spaces
(the assign Toaster:).

Books whose fonts or brushes are not installed ask, for each one,
whether to replace it with another (as the Toaster did); a brush left
unreplaced shows as a box.

Help shows the keys. The function keys are the Toaster's: F1 shadow
type, F2 light direction, F3 shadow length, F4 border, F5 shadow/border
in front, F6 justify, F7 render the page to disk, F8 and F9 render, F10
Take. Esc, the close gadget or Switcher leave the CG and ask first when
the book has changed.


Take and F7 without Toaster hardware
------------------------------------
Take plays the page in the CG's window: scrolls and crawls move at the
NTSC field rate (59.94 fields a second, kept by the clock); a key or click
stops it. F7 renders the page to a 24-bit IFF ILBM (the frame's size,
see-through parts of a key page in its mask), which Load RGB takes back
as a background.


Pages for the Switcher
----------------------
Save Page (in Page Commands), or SAVE_PAGE <file> from ARexx, writes the
current page as a crouton: its picture, an icon of it, and the kind of
page it is (framestore, key, scroll or crawl) with its speed, its time
and whether it loops or ends frozen. The Video Toaster Switcher for AROS
(0.7 and later) adds such a file to a project with Tools, "Add
croutons...", and puts it on air: a key page keyed over Program, a
scroll or crawl moving over it by the clock, a framestore page as a
still in its Framestore. A scroll or crawl is saved as the whole strip
of its lines, so a long one makes a large file.


ARexx
-----
The CG's port is CG_AREXX, with the functions of the Toaster CG
(GET_ SET_ PICK MAKE KILL LOAD SAVE REQ_ REND REXX MACR ...), answering
as the original did, so the Toaster's own scripts work unchanged. From
a script:

    call addlib('CG_AREXX', 0)
    call SET_PAGE(4)
    say GET_PAGE(TYPE)

From the shell, without an interpreter:

    cgrx SET_PAGE 4
    cgrx GET_PAGE TYPE

The Toaster's cg-config assigns its scripts to Alt-F1..F10 and
Shift-Alt-F1..F10 (see Running).


What differs from the Toaster CG
--------------------------------
- The interface is the Toaster's own art and lettering, read from your
  Toaster's CG and redrawn as clean outlines, so it stays sharp at any
  scale; text on the page is set by
  FreeType with full antialiasing (the Toaster's renderer used four
  levels). ToasterFonts (colour fonts) are decoded from the NTSC samples
  they hold.
- Requesters are reqtools'.
- Take shows the page in the window, and buffer pages show the picture
  loaded with Load RGB: the CG does not yet put a page on a running
  Switcher's Program itself. A page gets there as a file (see Pages for
  the Switcher).


Known issues in this release candidate
--------------------------------------
- Load Draw makes PostScript graphics (text in the current PostScript
  font, rotated and sheared); Encapsulated PostScript files cannot be
  loaded (the Toaster ran them through its PostScript interpreter). The
  port keeps a graphic's rotation and shear inside its text's buffer; the
  Toaster CG reads such a book but shows the graphic upright.
- F8 (Render line) shows the whole page, as F9 does.
- Return and the down arrow differ: Return goes to the next line or
  makes one, Down only moves. In the Toaster both were one function,
  whose source is not in NewTek's release.
- The Japanese and Korean front end (Ctrl, FEP_ functions) is not there.
- ARexx: macros run without the CG waiting for them; SET_CHAR takes one
  byte; GET_BACK gives no address.
- F7's file format and Font Clear's rule (a font or brush still used on
  a page is not removed) are inferred: their code is not in the release,
  only their names and messages.


Where it comes from, and the licence
------------------------------------
The CG's logic was converted from the Video Toaster CG source NewTek
released as the Open Video Toaster. What is NewTek's in it stays
NewTek's, on the terms of that release; nothing of a Video Toaster
installation is in this archive - the panel's art and font are read from
yours.

The AROS port, its renderer, the outline tracer and the tools are by
terminills, copyright (C) 2026, under the AROS Public License 1.1 (the
file LICENSE, or http://www.aros.org/license.html). The SVG renderer is
nanosvg by Mikko Mononen (zlib licence, see nanosvg-LICENSE.txt).

This is a release candidate, without warranty of any kind.
